.TH "cbor::lazy_entries" 3 "Version 0.9.0" "mbedded-cbor" \" -*- nroff -*-
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
cbor::lazy_entries \- The pairs of one map, from \fBcbor::lazy::entries()\fP, in the order of the bytes\&.  

.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.PP
\fR#include <cbor/lazy\&.hpp>\fP
.SS "Public Member Functions"

.in +1c
.ti -1c
.RI "iterator \fBbegin\fP () const"
.br
.ti -1c
.RI "std::default_sentinel_t \fBend\fP () const"
.br
.ti -1c
.RI "std::uint64_t \fBsize\fP () const"
.br
.ti -1c
.RI "void \fBfront\fP () const =delete"
.br
.in -1c
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP 
The pairs of one map, from \fBcbor::lazy::entries()\fP, in the order of the bytes\&. 

It is a forward range, a sized range and a borrowed range\&. \fRsize()\fP gives the count of pairs in the head\&. \fR*it\fP gives the key in \fRfirst\fP and the value in \fRsecond\fP, or the error of the pair\&. An iterator holds the owner of the bytes, so it stays valid after the lazy and the view end\&. \fRfront()\fP does not compile\&. When the iterator cannot step over a key or a value, \fR*it\fP gives \fRtoo_little_data\fP or another error of the bytes, and each later position gives that error\&. The view gives at most \fRsize()\fP values\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
message: {"a": 1, "b": 2, "c": 3}
call: auto const view = *doc\->entries()
result: lazy_entries of 3 pairs: "a": 1, "b": 2, "c": 3
call: view\&.size()
result: 3
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
    // bytes: a std::string with {"a": 1, "b": 2, "c": 3}
    auto const map = cbor::lazy::adopt(std::move(bytes));
    auto const view = *map\->entries();                                // 3 pairs
    auto const rest = std::ranges::distance(view | std::views::drop(1));  // 2
.fi
.PP
 
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBcbor::lazy::entries()\fP 

.PP
\fBcbor::lazy_elements\fP 
.RE
.PP

